# Written by `novo pkg init`.  Everything named here is something
# the toolchain writes into this directory and never publishes:
# the archive is an allow-list (novo.toml, src/, README.md,
# LICENSE, LICENSE-APACHE, CHANGELOG.md, examples/, assets/), so
# none of it can ship, and `novo pkg publish` does not count any
# of it against the clean-tree check either.

# Build intermediates — the LLVM IR, the object files and a
# generated linker script.  `novo build`, `novo pkg build` and
# `novo pkg test` all write here.
_novo/

# The generated API page.  `novo doc` writes it here so you can
# read what the registry will serve; the registry gets its own
# copy, uploaded beside the archive by `novo pkg publish`.  It is
# generated from the sources, so committing it would be
# committing a second original.
APIDOC.md

# The built binary — `novo pkg build` names it after the package.
# A library builds none; the line is here for when you add a
# `main` and it does.  Rename it with the package.
kafka-nv

# A device build (`novo build --target=<board>`) writes its image
# and its siblings into the directory the build was started in —
# the same place a host build puts `{pkg}`.  `-o <path>` sends
# them somewhere else.
*.elf
*.hex
*.bin
*.map

# NOT novo.lock.  A locked build resolves the same closure twice
# and does it offline once the cache is warm, and a consumer never
# reads a dependency's lock — they resolve their own closure and
# write their own — so committing it costs nobody anything and
# makes this repository's CI reproducible.  It is not on the
# publish allow-list, so it never ships either way, and
# `novo pkg publish` does not count it against the clean-tree
# check.

# Editor state.  Not a build artefact and not exempt from the
# clean-tree check — a stray one is still yours.
.vscode/settings.json.user
*.swp
